## Symmetric mixture: shell concentration is radial, separation is directional

For $X = S\Delta e_1 + Z$, the distribution is bimodal, but the bimodality is not primarily radial. Since

$$\|X\|^2 = \Delta^2 + \|Z\|^2 + 2S\Delta\, e_1^\top Z,$$

we get $\mathbb{E}\|X\|^2 = d + \Delta^2$ and $\operatorname{Var}(\|X\|^2) = 2d + 4\Delta^2$. The norm still concentrates, and the cloud still lives near a shell of radius $\sim\sqrt{d+\Delta^2}$. But the mixture separation lies along a specific direction, $e_1$. That is the central distinction: shell concentration is radial, mixture separation is directional.

::: {.narrow-figure}

::: {#cell-fig-mixture-small .cell execution_count=5}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Symmetric Gaussian mixture in dimensions 2, 5, and 20. The first row shows the true cloud for $d=2$ and a radius-only shell proxy for $d>2$; the second row shows a random 2D projection; the third shows PCA. The shell persists, but a random projection can hide the bimodality.](index_files/figure-html/fig-mixture-small-output-1.png){#fig-mixture-small width=705 height=567}
:::
:::


:::

::: {.narrow-figure}

::: {#cell-fig-mixture-large .cell execution_count=6}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Symmetric Gaussian mixture in dimensions 50 and 200. The cloud remains shell-like in radius, but the bimodal structure becomes hard to see in a random 2D projection and is much clearer in PCA.](index_files/figure-html/fig-mixture-large-output-1.png){#fig-mixture-large width=474 height=562}
:::
:::


:::

The mixed first row is deliberate: in two dimensions the true cloud shows the two blobs directly; in higher dimensions the shell proxy isolates radial concentration and therefore suppresses directional information by construction.

## Why random projections hide the mixture

Under a 2D projection with orthonormal columns $U$,

$$Y = U^\top X \sim \tfrac{1}{2}\mathcal{N}(+\Delta U^\top e_1, I_2) + \tfrac{1}{2}\mathcal{N}(-\Delta U^\top e_1, I_2).$$

The visible separation is controlled by $\Delta\|U^\top e_1\|$. For a random 2D subspace,

$$\mathbb{E}\|U^\top e_1\|^2 = \frac{2}{d},$$

so the apparent signal is typically of order $\Delta\sqrt{2/d}$ — which decays as dimension grows.

::: {.narrow-figure}

::: {#cell-fig-random-projection-signal .cell execution_count=7}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Repeated random 2D projections of the mixture. The visible projected signal decays like $\Delta \sqrt{2/d}$, so random low-dimensional views become less informative as dimension grows.](index_files/figure-html/fig-random-projection-signal-output-1.png){#fig-random-projection-signal width=584 height=342}
:::
:::


:::

## Why PCA helps in this model

For this mixture, $\mathbb{E}[X] = 0$ and $\operatorname{Cov}(X) = I_d + \Delta^2 e_1 e_1^\top$. The leading population principal component is exactly $e_1$, with top eigenvalue $1+\Delta^2$. PCA aligns with the signal because the signal direction is also the dominant variance direction.

::: {.narrow-figure}

::: {#cell-fig-signal-vs-random-1d .cell execution_count=8}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![One-dimensional projections of the mixture. Projection onto the signal coordinate remains bimodal, while projection onto a random direction becomes progressively less informative in higher dimension.](index_files/figure-html/fig-signal-vs-random-1d-output-1.png){#fig-signal-vs-random-1d width=683 height=265}
:::
:::


:::

## Takeaway

A high-dimensional dataset can be shell-concentrated in full space, look structureless in a random 2D projection, and reveal strong structure under a data-adaptive projection — all at once. That's why 2D plots in high dimension need interpretation, not just inspection.

